Hello All Things Additive team,

a workflow story that runs from an imported file to a print-ready 3MF.

Solidon3D takes a downloaded model, recognises its features — holes, slots,
countersinks, bosses, threads, and now patterns such as honeycombs and knurls
— and lets them be edited without CAD knowledge. Version 0.5.0 closes the gap
that made the workflow one-way: the result is now an exact body. Primitives
are built with real faces and edges, features stay exact through moving,
duplicating, rotating and removing, and STEP goes in and comes back out
cleanly.

The downstream half is where it may fit your coverage:

* Filament colours stay on exact bodies and follow every new meshing, so a
  multi-material part survives editing.
* Fits are checked on the real bodies in their assembled position, and the
  export can be cancelled before it runs.
* Exact bodies are meshed for the target printer — for a resin device as
  finely as its pixels require, and the check report names the value.
* The shape deviation map shows which faces of a meshing lie how far from the
  original.
* Handover to common slicers is direct, and the file can also be opened in a
  resin vendor tool whose settings Solidon does not know.

In this release the printing side also gained resin printers proper: two
generic devices by build volume, plus user-defined devices with pixel size and
minimum wall. A resin project no longer receives advice about nozzles, brims
or bridges.

70 user-visible changes in this release; 896 since the first 0.1 demo.

Solidon3D is an independent development from Germany, desktop for Windows,
macOS and Linux, one-time purchase, fully usable offline after a single
activation.

